  "evidence": [
    {
      "id": "E1",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Lexical methods (including full-text search) match tokens precisely. They only succeed when the query and document share vocabulary.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 0,
        "char_start": 451,
        "char_end": 583
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E2",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Lexical search has been part of information systems for decades.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1052,
        "char_end": 1116
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E3",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Early workloads depended on deterministic matching.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1117,
        "char_end": 1168
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E4",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Most queries issued by databases and other internal tools referenced exact terms like IDs or error codes.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1169,
        "char_end": 1274
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E5",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "For example, a SQL query using LIKE '%error%' searches for any text containing the substring \"error\".",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1275,
        "char_end": 1376
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E6",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Modern lexical search is much more advanced and determines relevance based on token presence and frequency.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1377,
        "char_end": 1484
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E7",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Documents are scored higher when query terms appear more frequently or are rarer across the entire corpus.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1485,
        "char_end": 1591
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E8",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Most lexical search implementations use inverted indexes.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 2,
        "char_start": 1622,
        "char_end": 1679
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E9",
      "source_id": "S3",
      "quote": "Semantic methods capture intended meaning, but are poorly suited for exact identifiers (IDs, numbers, proper names) that lexical systems handle precisely.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 0,
        "char_start": 586,
        "char_end": 740
      },
      "content_hash": "e3a02b0a34f704c0",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q1"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E10",
      "source_id": "S4",
      "quote": "Lexical search matches a query against an index of stored text.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 943,
        "char_end": 1006
      },
      "content_hash": "0af26eecab9cb002",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E11",
      "source_id": "S4",
      "quote": "Enterprises usually use lexical retrieval to search structured data such as product codes, identifiers, or technical documentation where the user is looking for an exact keyword.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1316,
        "char_end": 1494
      },
      "content_hash": "0af26eecab9cb002",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E12",
      "source_id": "S4",
      "quote": "First, data needs to be analyzed and undergo tokenization, where raw text is broken down into individual basic terms, followed by normalization techniques such as stemming or lemmatization.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 1,
        "char_start": 1495,
        "char_end": 1684
      },
      "content_hash": "0af26eecab9cb002",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E13",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Hybrid search is a powerful addition to many apps, but it isn't always the right choice.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 11,
        "char_start": 8728,
        "char_end": 8816
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E14",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Higher resource usage: You're maintaining two indexes (full-text and vector), which means more storage and memory usage.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 11,
        "char_start": 8896,
        "char_end": 9016
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E15",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Added latency: There’s more overhead when you’re running two searches in parallel and fusing results.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 11,
        "char_start": 9019,
        "char_end": 9120
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E16",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Tuning complexity: It takes some experimenting to balance keyword and semantic results.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 11,
        "char_start": 9231,
        "char_end": 9318
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E17",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Overkill for simple use cases: If your users only search by exact product IDs or your content is highly structured, pure keyword search is often enough.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 12,
        "char_start": 9470,
        "char_end": 9622
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E18",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "Similarly, if your queries are always conceptual with no exact-match requirements, vector search alone is probably simpler.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 12,
        "char_start": 9623,
        "char_end": 9746
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E19",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "These trade-offs are manageable for most production apps, especially when you use infrastructure that handles both indexing methods natively.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 12,
        "char_start": 9747,
        "char_end": 9888
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "E20",
      "source_id": "S5",
      "quote": "The key is knowing when the relevance improvements justify the added complexity.",
      "locator": {
        "chunk_index": 12,
        "char_start": 9889,
        "char_end": 9969
      },
      "content_hash": "b957b6ef72288b63",
      "question_ids": [
        "Q2"
      ]
    }
  ],
